# git ls-files --others --exclude-from=.git/info/exclude
# Lines that start with '#' are comments.
# For a project mostly in C, the following would be a good set of
# exclude patterns (uncomment them if you want to use them):
# *.[oa]
# *~
.*.sw[a-z]
vobs/projects/springboard/fos_linux/linux-yocto/SWBD*
vobs/projects/springboard/dist/bsnfosbuild
crave.conf
crave_logs/
cli_to_rest_mapping.tar.gz
coverity/.coverity_validated
_full_cov_output*
*.code-workspace
.vscode/
run-as-me.sh
.cfg
.cache/
compile_commands.json
node_modules
# *.asan/
*.orig
*.rej
__pycache__/
**/.claude/settings.local.json

# raven42/binfiles (private) owns everything under ~/bin, wherever it physically lives (rc/, private/, and
# the flat work-tier scripts alike) -- this repo must never track any of it.
/bin/
# ~/.rc/20-team, 30-user, and prompt.d are symlinks into ~/bin/rc/... (see the dotfiles reorg plan). /bin/
# above already covers their real content; these cover the symlinks themselves, which live directly under
# $HOME outside of ~/bin. No trailing slash: git doesn't treat a symlink-to-a-directory as a directory for
# gitignore matching purposes, so a trailing-slash pattern silently fails to match it.
# /.rc/20-team
# /.rc/30-user
# /.rc/prompt.d


# Private profile directory symlinks owned by binfiles.
# /.rc/30-work
# /.rc/31-home

# Machine-local shell selection links.
# /.rc/20-team
# /.rc/30-user
# /.rc/prompt.d

# Selected private tiers.
# /.rc/10-profile
# /.rc/30-team
# /.rc/40-user
!/.rc/**